schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "What Is a Knowledge Commons?"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    A plain-language guide for small teams comfortable with docs/GitHub on
    building a shared knowledge base that survives team turnover. Distinguishes
    a "commons" from a plain wiki by governance (drawing on Elinor Ostrom's
    commons-governance principles and her later "knowledge as a commons" work
    with Charlotte Hess), argues turnover is the real test of a knowledge base,
    profiles three real working examples at different scales (Wikipedia,
    SuperBenefit Knowledge Garden, GravityDAO), introduces the "digital garden"
    posture (rough/unfinished notes published openly rather than perfected
    before publishing), and warns against building an ontology before content
    exists.
  audience: small teams and local nodes new to knowledge-commons practice
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Perfectionism vs. "digital garden" posture — the article argues pages
      must be allowed to be rough/unfinished and openly marked as such, rather
      than withheld until polished.
    - >-
      Structure-first vs. content-first — explicitly argues against designing
      a full ontology before a real pile of notes exists to inform it.
  related_concepts:
    - knowledge-commons
    - digital-garden
  related_resources:
    - superbenefit-knowledge-garden
    - gravitydao
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-knowledge-commons.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
# - Did not create separate resource cards for Obsidian, Logseq, or TiddlyWiki (tooling
#   mentioned only as a supporting list of digital-garden-compatible tools, not load-bearing
#   named examples the article builds an argument around, unlike SuperBenefit/GravityDAO/
#   Wikipedia).
# - Murmurations Protocol appears only in the References list, not discussed in body text —
#   captured as a resource anyway since it is directly relevant to this framework's own
#   federation concerns, but flagged as thin extraction (reference-only mention).
